  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and humid conditions will persist through \nThursday with daily of risk of showers and thunderstorms, some\nof which, could be severe.\n\nAs of 9 PM, a well organized MCS is ongoing across northwestern\nportions of Ohio. Locally, a pocket of drier air remains in\nplace aloft, as shown by the 00z IAD sounding. This drier\nprofile limited the development of thunderstorms this afternoon\nto Central Virginia. Deeper moisture is expected to gradually \nreturn northeastward overnight into the day tomorrow ahead of a\nshortwave disturbance and associated area of low pressure that \nare forecast to pass well to our north. The aforementioned MCS\nis expected to outrun the deeper moisture over the next few\nhours, and eventually show signs of weakening as it works into\nwestern PA and northern WV. Given the presence of a pretty well\nestablished cold pool, it could potentially make it all the way\nto the Allegheny Front after midnight in a much weakened state \nbefore dissipating. Recent HRRR runs also indicate that some\nadditional development of thunderstorms may be possible late\ntonight behind this MCS across western MD as deeper moisture\nadvects into the area from the southwest. If those storms were \nto develop, they would likely be scattered in nature and \nelevated, posing little threat for severe weather.\n\nAnother MCS is forecast to develop near the southern end of Lake\nMichigan later tonight and then dive east-southeastward into \nthe Ohio Valley late tonight into tomorrow morning. Depending \non how this MCS evolves it could either maintain itself across \nour forecast area tomorrow, or have its remnant outflow focus \nthe development of new convection tomorrow afternoon. If the MCS\nwere to totally fall apart before crossing the mountains, with \nno remnant cold pool making it further east, the development of \nthunderstorms across our area would be a bit more in question, \ngiven only weak large scale forcing for ascent and questions \nabout where/if a lee trough would set up. What does appear clear\nis that we should see strong daytime heating ahead of whatever \noccurs, with the potential remnant outflow and disturbance aloft\nmoving through at peak heating. Before storms form, temperatures  \nare forecast to make it into the low to mid 90s, which when \ncombined with dewpoints in the lower 70s will make it feel like \naround 100. In terms of timing, storms are expected to move \nthrough from west to east between roughly 1 and 8 PM. Any storms\ntomorrow should be capable of producing damaging wind gusts. \nPrevious discussion follows below...\n\nA hot and humid air mass will persist over the mid-Atlantic \nregion between a subtropical ridge over the south central U.S. \nand an active northern stream jet. As a result of this active \nnorthern stream flow, numerous shortwave-troughs are fcst to \ntraverse the area during much of the work week keeping the risk\nof daily showers and thunderstorms, some of which, could be \nsevere.\n\nTue and Wed look like the most active days of the week due to \nstronger height falls and stronger mid-level flow with Tue\nlooking the day with greater instability and more enhanced\nsevere wx risk. As always, exact timing and location of severe\nweather will depend on location's of outflow boundaries from the \nprevious day.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler conditions are expected for the end of the \nweek, although showers and thunderstorms remain possible at times.\n\nThe southward progress of the cold front becomes more uncertain\nfor the end of the week, although it likely drifts south with \ntime. Both physics-based and AI guidance shows lower severe \nweather chances Thursday with declining instability. Showers and\nthunderstorms could remain possible though with the front \nnearby. For Friday and Saturday in particular, there may be a \nbetter chance of drying out as high pressure builds to the \nnorth. This will also bring a corresponding cool down in \ntemperatures (potentially a bit below normal) along with lower \nhumidity. The front won't be too far to the south however, and \nwill likely attempt to lift north this weekend or early next \nweek as a low pressure system moves into the Great Lakes. Thus, \nsome low end PoPs remain in the forecast each day through the \nend of the forecast period.",
